(vector
 (map
  (:adr/id "adr-2609112800-presence-is-not-verification-and-the-digest-beside-it-was-never-checked")
  (:adr/title "Presence is not verification, and the digest beside it was never checked")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-11")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2609110200-a-moving-defcid-reaches-the-conformance-corpus-and-no-deployment-lock"))
  (:adr/body "
## What this iteration set out to do

Re-measure gap 02's second candidate: the blast radius of a moving DefCID,
the count baked into locks, manifests and cache keys. Several recent ADRs
have asserted that v3 moves every DefCID while citing an older count, so
the count was worth re-deriving rather than repeating.

## It was already answered, and better

adr-2609110200 measured it on 2026-09-10 and landed the result: the reach
is the language conformance corpus and it stops there; zero of the
candidates is a deployment lock, an app manifest or a capability. It also
records the two traps -- that every dag-cbor CID shares the prefix so app
and graph CIDs would be counted as DefCIDs, and that the files named
contract-lock.edn and invalid_definition_cid_lock.edn are conformance
fixtures rather than locks -- and it reports a RANGE rather than a single
number, because one regex arm undercounted and reporting either alone
would present an artefact of the regex as a property of the corpus.

Today's scan, cut differently, reproduces that conclusion independently.
Recorded as a replication, not as a new answer.

## What the different cut did add

The earlier ADR cut by whether a file names a DefCID key. This one cut by
whether a file is COMMITTED:

    build outputs (untracked)   35 files
    committed                   10 files

and of the committed ones, every path but one is a conformance fixture or
a test fixture. The exception is a shipped artifact's provenance:
network-awai/cloud-murakumo-cli, the provider kernel.

## Is that a lock? No, and the reason is worth keeping

The rule is that finding a mechanism is not finding a working one, so the
question is what READS it. Three readers exist, and all three check the
same thing:

    provenance present / ABSENT

Presence, not content. Nothing verifies the DefCIDs inside it, so the
provenance is a receipt rather than a binding and the earlier ADR's zero
stands.

## But the digest beside it was recorded and never checked

The publication set records a sha256 for every file it ships, including
that provenance. Nothing compared them. Measured: both agree today -- the
good case, and precisely the case in which an absent check is invisible.

That is the gap this iteration closed. `verify-kernel` now compares every
digest the publication set names, reports a COUNT of mismatches rather
than a boolean, and REFUSES with exit 2 when the set cannot be read, so a
check that could not run does not look like one that ran and found
nothing.

## Why it belongs to gap 02

It makes the blast radius detectable instead of silent. If a payload
change moves the DefCIDs in that provenance, its bytes move with them; the
kernel would still load, still pass its arithmetic self-check, and still
report `provenance present`. Now the digest check goes red and names the
file.

So the v3 answer is unchanged and better supported: nothing deployed pins
a DefCID, and the one deployed artifact that carries them will now say so
if they move.

## Reproduce

    cd <cloud-murakumo-cli worktree>
    nbb scripts/verify-kernel.cljs        # 0 clean, 1 failures, 2 refused

Seen red both ways:

    move one DefCID inside the provenance  -> exit 1, names the file and
                                              both digests, 1 mismatch
    remove the publication set             -> exit 2 REFUSED, not 0

## What is not claimed

No new blast-radius number. adr-2609110200 remains the measurement of
record and its range is not restated here. io-ipld stays test-only.
")))
